                <pb facs="0187" n="81" sameAs="frus:frus1891/pg81"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Pacheco to Mr. Blaine</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States in Guatemala and
                                Honduras</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1891-09-15"><hi rendition="#italic">September 15,
                        1891</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Pacheco telegraphs text of articles 253 and 257 of the navigation and marine
                    law of Salvador, which fixes penalty on vessels leaving port without clearance
                    papers as follows: <list>
                        <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">Art</hi>. 253. No vessel ought to set sail
                            without the license of the commandant of the port under penalty of
                            confiscation.</item>
                        <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">Art</hi>. 257. Every infraction of the
                            articles contained in this paragraph shall he punishable by fine not
                            exceeding $200, at discretion of the commandant of the port.</item>
                    </list></p>
            </div>
        </body>
